<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE bindings PUBLIC "-//NetBeans//DTD Editor KeyBindings settings 1.1//EN"
                          "http://www.netbeans.org/dtds/EditorKeyBindings-1_1.dtd">
<!--
    3.1.0: Cmd+D adds the next occurrence to the selection, as it does in
    VS Code. The platform already has the gesture (addCaretSelectNext, on
    D-J in NetBeans-keybindings.xml); this puts the switcher's chord on it
    too, and D-J keeps working.

    Registered where the chord is free, the rule 3.1.0 stated: a
    profile's own meaning of a chord wins there - the same rule the
    Keymaps side keeps for DS-E. The defaults module binds D-D in Eclipse
    (remove-line, Eclipse's own Ctrl+D) and NetBeans55 (shift-line-left).
    3.2's census corrected 3.1.0's measurement, which had also called the
    chord free in Emacs and Idea: Emacs binds it on every OS (M-D, kill
    word, in its macOS file - M is Cmd there - and C-D, delete character,
    on Windows and Linux, where D-D is Ctrl+D), and Idea binds Ctrl+D to
    Duplicate Line. So this file rides the NetBeans profile on every OS
    and the Idea profile on macOS only (its layer registration carries
    nbeditor-settings-targetOS=OS_MAC there). KeymapProfileParityTest holds
    the profiles and VsCodeKeymapResolutionTest resolves the chord.
-->
<bindings>
    <bind actionName="addCaretSelectNext" key="D-D"/>
</bindings>
